      body: 'Deacon Marcus Hill and Brother Andre Lyles had not spoken in three weeks, and the whole men’s fellowship at Greater New Hope knew it. It started over the youth basketball budget. Andre had said something sharp in the meeting, Marcus had taken it personally, and since then each man had been telling his side to anybody who would listen. Marcus told his wife, his brother-in-law and two ushers. Andre told the group chat. By the third Sunday the men were choosing seats by side. On Monday night Marcus sat in his truck after work with his phone in his hand, halfway through typing one more message about Andre to one more brother. Then he remembered what the pastor had played for the men on Saturday morning, an old radio sermon by a Louisville preacher, Stephen Darby. Darby had said a man should be able to sit down with another man and say, this is how I feel, this is what I think, and settle it without getting everybody involved. The pastor had read Jesus’ own words from the Gospel of Matthew: "Moreover if thy brother shall trespass against thee, go and tell him his fault between thee and him alone: if he shall hear thee, thou hast gained thy brother." (Matthew 18:15) Marcus deleted the message. He drove to the church, where he knew Andre would be locking up the gym. Andre saw him coming and folded his arms. "You here to fuss?" he asked. "No," Marcus said. "I’m here to talk to you instead of about you. That should have happened three weeks ago." They stood by the truck for forty minutes. Andre admitted he had been embarrassed in front of the other men. Marcus admitted he had been telling the story wrong on purpose. Neither of them got everything he wanted for the budget. But on Sunday they sat together, and when the group chat lit up with somebody else’s complaint, Andre typed one line: "Go talk to him, brother. Face to face. It works."',
