      body: 'Kevin Bryant had been a minister at his church for six years, and he could pray longer and louder than any man there. What he could not do was keep a job. Every few months there was a new reason: the Lord was moving him, the boss was against the anointing, the season had changed. His wife, Tasha, worked doubles at the hospital and paid the light bill. When the landlord came, Kevin sent her to the door. When their son was suspended, Kevin said he was fasting and could not go to the school, so Tasha went. Slowly she began making every decision, and Kevin let her, and then he resented her for it. One night their pastor asked Kevin to stay after Bible study. He did not preach at him. He opened to the story of Naboth’s vineyard and read it slowly, the king who went home and lay on his bed and would not eat because a man had told him no, and the queen who wrote letters in his name and sealed them with his seal. Then the pastor read the verse that lays the blame where it belongs: "But there was none like unto Ahab, which did sell himself to work wickedness in the sight of the LORD, whom Jezebel his wife stirred up." (1 Kings 21:25) "Kevin," he said, "Pastor Darby used to say men trade their responsibility for spirituality. Your wife is not your problem. She is carrying what you set down." Kevin was quiet a long time. Then the pastor turned two more pages and showed him that when Ahab tore his clothes and humbled himself, Yahweh saw it and showed mercy. "It is not too late," the pastor said. Kevin took a warehouse job that Monday. It was not glamorous. He kept it. Six months later, for the first time in years, he was the one who answered the door.',
