      title: 'Ten Years of Conferences',
      body: 'Denise Carter could tell you the name of every speaker at every women’s conference in the Southeast for ten years running. She traveled to them on credit cards, came home with new prophetic words about her season and her breakthrough, and threw herself into her church’s women’s ministry three and four nights a week. Her husband, Curtis, drove a city bus and coached their son’s little league team. At first he asked her gently to stay home more. Then he asked her to stop telling their business in prayer circles. Then he stopped asking. When he finally moved out, Denise’s sisters at church told her the Lord was removing what was hindering her calling. She believed them for a while. It was her daughter, at sixteen, who said the thing nobody else would say. "Mama, you were at church every night, and Daddy was the one who came to my games." Denise did not sleep that night. In the morning she found her old Bible and read the passage her mother had underlined years before: "Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh." (Genesis 2:24) She called Curtis. She did not ask him to come back. She told him he had been right, and she was sorry. Then she called the women’s ministry leader and stepped down for a season. Curtis did not come home that year. But he came to dinner on Sundays, and then on Wednesdays, and they began to talk in a way they had not talked since their son was born. The road back was long. Denise says now that the gospel bond and the marriage covenant were always meant to be stronger than any sisterhood, and that the strongest sisters are the ones who send you home.',
      verse: 'Genesis 2:24',
