    'Pastor Darby argues that a generation of boys has been nurtured to death: comforted, defended and excused, but never corrected by a father, so they grow up strong in body and weak in spirit, unable to take a man’s correction or carry pressure. He traces it through Eden, through slavery’s assault on the Black family, through welfare rules that pushed fathers out, and through his own childhood among his mother’s boyfriends and the streets. His call is to bring fathers and godly men back into boys’ lives, to discipline and not only comfort, to stop teaching children that their fathers are no good, and to forgive.',
