      title: 'Uncle Ray and the Lawn Mower',
      body: 'Tamika Lewis had raised her son Jaylen alone since he was two, and she had done it well. He was polite, he made decent grades, and he called her every day from his friend’s house. But at thirteen something changed. Jaylen cried when his coach yelled. He quit the team after three weeks. He argued with every teacher who corrected him, and every time, Tamika went up to the school and argued for him. One Sunday her older brother Ray, a deacon who ran a small landscaping business, watched Jaylen sulk through dinner because his video game had been taken away. After the dishes Ray said quietly, "Let me have him on Saturdays this summer." Tamika bristled. Then she remembered the sermon her pastor had played the week before, an old radio message by Pastor Stephen Darby called Nurtured to Death, and the Scripture he had read from Hebrews about chastening that does not feel joyous but afterward yields fruit. She said yes. The first Saturday Jaylen came home sunburned and furious. The second Saturday he came home tired. By July he was pushing a mower by himself and arguing with Uncle Ray about the best way to edge a sidewalk, and losing, and laughing about it. In August Ray paid him for the whole summer and told him in front of his mother, "You finished. Most men your age don’t." Jaylen stood up a little straighter. When school started he went out for the team again. The coach yelled. Jaylen did not cry. He said, "Yes sir," and ran the drill again. Tamika told her pastor later that she had not loved her son any less by letting a man correct him. She had loved him more. "Now no chastening for the present seemeth to be joyous, but grievous: nevertheless afterward it yieldeth the peaceable fruit of righteousness unto them which are exercised thereby." (Hebrews 12:11)',
