      body: 'Every time Brandon Mitchell got in trouble, his mother, Sheila, went to the school and fought for him. When he was nine and pushed a classmate, she said the other boy started it. When he was twelve and cursed a teacher, she said the teacher was prejudiced. When he was fifteen and stopped going to class, she said the school was failing him. Some of that was true. Most of it was not, and Sheila knew it, but Brandon’s father had left and she had promised herself that her son would never feel alone. Brandon was nineteen when he was arrested. Sheila sat in the back of the courtroom and watched him stand before the judge with his head down, unable to speak for himself, and she understood something she had been refusing to see. She had comforted him for nineteen years, and no one had ever corrected him. That night she called her pastor. He did not condemn her. He read her a line from Proverbs: "The rod and reproof give wisdom: but a child left to himself bringeth his mother to shame." (Proverbs 29:15) "Sister," he said, "you were not wrong to love him. But love has two hands. One comforts and one corrects." He told her about a preacher named Stephen Darby who had once said that some boys are nurtured to death, and that the cure was not less love but a father’s kind of love too. Then he asked whether Brandon’s father was still living. He was. Sheila had not spoken to him in eleven years. It took her a month to make the call. She forgave him on the phone, out loud, for the first time. He started visiting the jail. He was not a perfect man, but he told Brandon the truth, and Brandon listened. When Brandon came home, his father helped him find work on a roofing crew. Sheila says now that the hardest prayer she ever prayed was for Yahweh to send somebody to tell her son no.',
