    'The quoted lines are his own words from the archive.org recording, each with its time; his longer arguments are summarized in prose. The text he reads aloud for much of the sermon is an account he attributes to an African brother he does not name, describing a spirit addressing its agents; it is taught here as the text he read, not as revelation. Stated plainly: the names, kingdoms and ranks of water spirits come from West African tradition and deliverance practice, not from Scripture; the record traces Mami Wata imagery to European mermaid and snake-charmer pictures; his account of water spirits as surviving hybrids from before the flood is not in the Word, which says only what Genesis 6 says. Weighed and corrected by the Word in the lesson: the claim in the text he read that only about fifty people out of more than a billion were saved in three years (Revelation 7:9 shows a multitude no one could number); his fear of eating food prepared by others (1 Timothy 4:4-5; 1 Corinthians 10:25). Not repeated here: claims about the ingredients of cosmetics and hair, about named entertainers and ministers, about climate science, and about a 2015 controversy over fetal tissue, none of which is the substance of his call and some of which the record does not support.',
