      title: 'The Man in the Silver Sedan',
      body: 'Elder James Porter had been fasting three days for his granddaughter, who was in the hospital, and he was tired. On the way home from visiting her, a man in a silver sedan cut him off on the expressway, then slowed down in front of him and made a rude gesture out the window. James felt it rise up in him, thirty years of Chicago traffic in his chest. He sped up to pull alongside. His wife, Lorraine, put her hand on his arm. "James," she said. "You just spent three days fasting. Don’t give it away for a silver Honda." He laughed in spite of himself. He eased off the gas. Then he remembered something from a sermon their pastor had played for the prayer team, an old message by a Louisville preacher named Stephen Darby. Darby had told almost the same story about himself. A man had cut him off, then nearly run into his car, and his flesh wanted to ram the man and let the insurance sort it out. Instead, Darby said, he put on his brakes and prayed, forgive me Lord, bless him. And Darby had said the enemy loves to wait until you are tired and then poke you, because if he can make you angry he can steal your strength. James said it out loud in the car: "Lord, forgive me. Bless that man. Get him home safe." Lorraine said amen. The silver sedan took the next exit and was gone. James drove the rest of the way home humming, and when they got there, the phone was ringing. It was the hospital. Their granddaughter’s fever had broken. Lorraine looked at him and smiled. "See?" she said. "You kept your fast." That night James read the verse their pastor had quoted: "Bless them which persecute you: bless, and curse not." (Romans 12:14)',
      verse: 'Romans 12:14',
