  voice: VOICE,
  bigIdea:
    'Reading aloud the so-called Willie Lynch letter, Pastor Darby argues that Black Americans still live out a pattern of division planted in slavery: young against old, light against dark, women against men, every small difference made bigger, until a people distrusts itself, hates its own features, and reverses the roles of husband and wife. He calls it a chain on the mind, and his cure is instruction, identity, elders who impart, fathers who show up, marriages built on cooperation, and righteous living. The record shows the letter itself is a twentieth-century composition; the division he names, and the documented history behind it, are real, and the Word speaks to both.',
  inApp:
    'Hear the recording Willie Lynch Syndrome (Next Chapter Radio, 7 April 2016) on archive.org; his sermon begins at about 6:26, his reading of the letter at about 19:29, the list of differences at about 36:58, his counsel on marriage at about 95:45, and his closing text at about 113:03. Then read Proverbs 15:32, Matthew 12:25, Psalm 133:1, Ephesians 4:25, Exodus 21:16 and Acts 17:26, and weigh what he said by the Word: what holds without the letter, and what does the Word say breaks the chain?',
