    'The quoted lines are his own words from the archive.org recording, each with its time; his longer arguments are summarized in prose, and the letter text he reads is not quoted here. Stated plainly from the record: the Willie Lynch letter is a twentieth-century composition, in print since at least 1970 and spread widely in the 1990s, which historians including Roy Rosenzweig and William Jelani Cobb have shown to be a hoax by its anachronisms; Darby knew it was called a myth and argued from its fit to present life, and the lesson answers that argument by the Word and by documented records. Verified in the record and taught as real: that enslaved people could not legally marry and that sales broke up families; the Thirteenth Amendment’s exception for punishment for crime; the FBI’s COINTELPRO program and its own stated aims of dividing Black leaders. Weighed by the Word in the lesson: his identity teaching about the tribe of Judah (Acts 17:26; Galatians 3:28-29; Romans 2:29; Revelation 7:9); his reading of the two swords in Luke 22 (Luke 22:38; Matthew 26:52). Named once in the lesson and stated plainly as false: his claim that Jews owned the slave ships and run the entertainment industry; the American Historical Association in 1995 condemned the claim that Jews played a disproportionate role in the Atlantic slave trade, and historians such as Wim Klooster and Seymour Drescher found their role minimal; those lines are not quoted. Not repeated here, because the record does not support them and the Word contradicts them: claims in this sermon about the race of King James and of Cain, and about a 1974 government memorandum (NSSM 200, which concerns population growth in developing countries abroad).',
