      title: 'The Copy on the Refrigerator',
      body: 'For twenty years Brother Lamont Givens kept a photocopy of the Willie Lynch letter taped inside his kitchen cabinet. A man in the barbershop had given it to him in 1996, the year after the Million Man March, and it had explained everything: the crabs in a barrel, the light and dark, the men and women at war. He had read it aloud to his sons. Then his grandson, a history major at Howard, came home for Thanksgiving and saw it. "Granddad," he said gently, "you know that isn’t real, right?" He showed Lamont the words in it that no one used in 1712, and the historians who had traced where it came from. Lamont felt something like shame, then anger. He had taught it to his sons. Had he taught them a lie? He did not sleep well that night. In the morning his grandson brought him something else: the true record. The laws that would not let enslaved people marry. The records of families sold apart, a million people driven south. The amendment that ended slavery except as a punishment for crime. The FBI’s own memo about keeping Black leaders divided. "It happened, Granddad," the young man said. "It’s worse than the letter, because it’s real. We don’t need the fake one." Lamont sat with his Bible a long time that morning. He found the verse his pastor had preached at New Year’s: "Wherefore putting away lying, speak every man truth with his neighbour: for we are members one of another." (Ephesians 4:25) He took down the photocopy and threw it away. That Sunday he asked his pastor if he could teach the men’s class. He brought the real documents, and he brought the Word, and he told the men the truth: that a people does not have to lie to tell what was done to it, and that Yahweh can make a divided house one again.',
      verse: 'Ephesians 4:25',
